#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180676 :tutorial/content "Factorials can be applied to variables, too. To manipulate a factorial expression involving a variable, we just need to remember that the variable represents a number, and the previous number can be obtained by subtracting $1$ from the variable.\n\nSo, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5! & =5\\cdot \\overset{4!}{\\overset{⏞}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n! & =n\\cdot \\overset{(n-1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-1)\\cdot (n-2)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =n\\cdot (n-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes some positive integer.\n\nLikewise, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3!\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\nx! = x \\cdot (x-1) \\cdot (x-2)!\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d18ec8c1-f094-5d15-ac9e-61e003934f78" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3885 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
